    subgraph VIS["🖥️ Visualization"]
        GRAF["📊 Grafana<br/>dashboards"]
        LFUI["📊 Langfuse UI<br/>trace explorer"]
        IH["📊 Intelligence Hub<br/>(UI Console)"]
    end

    AGENT -->|"OTLP spans<br/>+ metrics"| OTEL
    TOOLS -->|"OTLP spans"| OTEL
    AGENT -->|"LLM traces<br/>+ scores"| LF
    UI -->|"page metrics"| OTEL

    OTEL -->|"remote write"| PROM
    OTEL -->|"log push"| LOKI2
    LF -->|"persist"| LFDB

    PROM --> GRAF
    LOKI2 --> GRAF
    LFDB --> LFUI
    LFUI -.->|"embedded"| IH
    GRAF -.->|"embedded"| IH

    classDef src fill:#7c3aed,stroke:#8b5cf6,color:#fff,rx:8
    classDef col fill:#d97706,stroke:#f59e0b,color:#fff,rx:8
    classDef store fill:#059669,stroke:#10b981,color:#fff,rx:8
    classDef vis fill:#0e7490,stroke:#06b6d4,color:#fff,rx:8

    class AGENT,TOOLS,UI src
    class OTEL,LF col
    class PROM,LOKI2,LFDB store
    class GRAF,LFUI,IH vis
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Memory Lifecycle</h2>
      <p>The dual-layer memory system manages conversation context across turns and sessions:</p>
      <div class="docs-diagram">
        <div class="mermaid">
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> NewMessage: User sends message
    NewMessage --> STMWrite: Save to conversations table
    STMWrite --> STMCheck: Check message count
    STMCheck --> UnderLimit: count lte 100
    STMCheck --> OverLimit: count gt 100
    OverLimit --> Truncate: Remove oldest messages
    Truncate --> UnderLimit
    UnderLimit --> SummaryCheck: Check summary threshold
    SummaryCheck --> NoUpdate: Below threshold
    SummaryCheck --> LTMUpdate: Above threshold
    LTMUpdate --> Summarize: LLM summarizes recent msgs
    Summarize --> WriteLTM: Update session_summaries
    WriteLTM --> [*]
    NoUpdate --> [*]
        </div>
      </div>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Storage</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Lifecycle</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>STM</strong></td><td><code>conversations</code> table</td><td>100 messages/session</td><td>FIFO — oldest dropped when limit reached</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>LTM</strong></td><td><code>session_summaries</code> table</td><td>2KB rolling/session</td><td>LLM-generated summary, updated periodically</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Knowledge</strong></td><td>ChromaDB collections</td><td>Unlimited</td><td>Persistent until explicit deletion</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Agent System ── -->
    <div id="doc-agents" class="docs-section">
      <div class="docs-breadcrumb"><a href="/docs">Docs</a> / Core Features</div>
      <h1>Agent System</h1>
      <p>The agent system uses the ReAct (Reasoning + Acting) pattern via LangGraph.</p>

      <h2>Agent Lifecycle</h2>
      <div class="docs-diagram">
        <div class="mermaid">
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> Receive: User prompt
    Receive --> InputGuardrails: Check safety
    InputGuardrails --> LoadContext: Pass
    InputGuardrails --> Blocked: Block
    LoadContext --> Reason: Memory + RAG
    Reason --> Act: Tool needed
    Act --> Observe: Tool result
    Observe --> Reason: Continue
    Reason --> OutputGuardrails: Final answer
    OutputGuardrails --> Respond: Pass/Flag
    Respond --> SaveMemory
    SaveMemory --> [*]
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Creating Agents</h2>
      <p>Use the <strong>Agent Builder</strong> page to create custom agents with:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Custom system prompts</li>
        <li>Tool selection (from registered tools)</li>
        <li>LLM provider and model configuration</li>
        <li>Memory settings</li>
        <li>Guardrail profiles</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Agent-to-Agent (A2A)</h2>
      <p>Agents can delegate tasks to other agents using the A2A protocol. Configure delegation rules on the A2A page.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── LLM Providers ── -->
    <div id="doc-llm" class="docs-section">
      <div class="docs-breadcrumb"><a href="/docs">Docs</a> / Core Features</div>
      <h1>LLM Providers</h1>
      <p>The platform supports 4 LLM providers with automatic fallback. A multi-provider LLM factory (<code>agent/llm.py</code>) abstracts provider differences behind a unified interface — agents never interact with provider APIs directly.</p>

      <h2>Provider Matrix</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Provider</th><th>Models</th><th>Type</th><th>API Keys Required</th><th>Configuration</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ollama</strong> (Default)</td><td>llama3, mistral, phi3</td><td>Local</td><td>None</td><td><code>OLLAMA_HOST</code> (default: <code>http://ollama:11434</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Azure OpenAI</strong></td><td>GPT-4o, GPT-4, GPT-3.5</td><td>Cloud</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY</code>, <code>AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>OpenAI</strong></td><td>GPT-4o, GPT-4, GPT-3.5</td><td>Cloud</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Azure AI Foundry</strong></td><td>Custom deployments</td><td>Cloud</td><td>Yes</td><td><code>AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_ENDPOINT</code>, <code>AZURE_AI_FOUNDRY_KEY</code></td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>Provider Selection &amp; Fallback</h2>
      <div class="docs-diagram">
        <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A["Agent run request<br/>(provider + model)"] --> B{"Selected provider<br/>available?"}
    B -->|"✅ Yes"| C["Use selected provider"]
    B -->|"❌ No"| D{"Try next provider<br/>in fallback chain"}
    D -->|"Available"| C
    D -->|"None available"| E["⛔ Return error"]
    C --> F["LangChain ChatModel<br/>(streaming)"]
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Users select the provider/model from the Run Agent page dropdown. If the selected provider is unreachable (network error, invalid key), the factory tries the next configured provider. Ollama is always available as a fallback since it runs locally.</p>

      <div class="docs-callout tip">
        <strong>Zero-config start:</strong> Ollama works out of the box with no API keys. The platform pulls <code>llama3</code> and <code>nomic-embed-text</code> models automatically. For cloud providers, set the appropriate environment variables in your <code>.env</code> file.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Memory & Knowledge ── -->
    <div id="doc-memory" class="docs-section">
      <div class="docs-breadcrumb"><a href="/docs">Docs</a> / Core Features</div>
      <h1>Memory &amp; Knowledge Base</h1>
      <p>The platform manages two distinct data systems: <strong>conversation memory</strong> (SQLite) for session context and <strong>knowledge base</strong> (ChromaDB) for document retrieval. They serve different purposes and never overlap.</p>

      <h2>Conversation Memory</h2>
      <p>SQLite-based dual-layer system that gives agents context about past interactions:</p>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Table</th><th>Capacity</th><th>Trigger</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Short-Term (STM)</strong></td><td><code>conversations</code></td><td>100 messages/session</td><td>Every message</td><td>Recent conversation context — injected into every LLM prompt</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Long-Term (LTM)</strong></td><td><code>session_summaries</code></td><td>2KB rolling/session</td><td>Message count threshold</td><td>LLM-generated compressed summary — provides continuity when STM overflows</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h3>Memory Lifecycle</h3>
      <div class="docs-diagram">
        <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A["New message"] --> B["Write to STM<br/>(conversations table)"]
    B --> C{"STM count<br/>&gt; 100?"}
    C -->|"No"| D{"Summary<br/>threshold?"}
    C -->|"Yes"| E["Drop oldest<br/>messages (FIFO)"]
    E --> D
    D -->|"No"| F["✅ Done"]
    D -->|"Yes"| G["LLM summarizes<br/>recent messages"]
    G --> H["Update LTM<br/>(session_summaries)"]
    H --> F
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Knowledge Base (RAG)</h2>
      <p>ChromaDB-powered vector store for document retrieval. Completely separate from conversation memory — used for augmenting LLM responses with domain-specific knowledge.</p>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Stage</th><th>Component</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Chunking</strong></td><td>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</td><td>chunk_size=1000, overlap=200, separators: \\n\\n, \\n, space</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Embedding</strong></td><td>nomic-embed-text (Ollama)</td><td>768-dimensional vectors, local inference</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Indexing</strong></td><td>ChromaDB HNSW</td><td>Approximate nearest neighbor, cosine distance metric</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Retrieval</strong></td><td>Similarity search</td><td>Top-k results injected into prompt context window</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Ingestion</strong></td><td>Documents page / n8n workflow</td><td>Supports PDF, TXT, MD file uploads</td></tr>
      </table>

      <div class="docs-callout info">
        <strong>Design Note:</strong> Conversation memory (SQLite) and knowledge base (ChromaDB) are intentionally separate. Memory tracks <em>what was said</em>; knowledge stores <em>what is known</em>. They converge only at prompt-build time, where both are injected into the LLM context.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Guardrails ── -->
    <div id="doc-guardrails" class="docs-section">
      <div class="docs-breadcrumb"><a href="/docs">Docs</a> / Core Features</div>
      <h1>Guardrails</h1>
      <p>The guardrail system enforces safety and quality controls on both inputs and outputs.</p>

      <h2>Categories</h2>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(2,1fr);gap:0.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
        <div style="background:var(--glass-bg);border:1px solid var(--glass-border);border-radius:8px;padding:0.75rem">
          <strong>🛡️ Content Safety</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.78rem;margin:0.25rem 0 0;color:var(--text-3)">Toxicity, hate speech, violence detection</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background:var(--glass-bg);border:1px solid var(--glass-border);border-radius:8px;padding:0.75rem">
          <strong>✨ Quality</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.78rem;margin:0.25rem 0 0;color:var(--text-3)">Hallucination, relevance, coherence checks</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background:var(--glass-bg);border:1px solid var(--glass-border);border-radius:8px;padding:0.75rem">
          <strong>⚖️ Fairness</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.78rem;margin:0.25rem 0 0;color:var(--text-3)">Bias detection, stereotyping prevention</p>
        </div>
        <div style="background:var(--glass-bg);border:1px solid var(--glass-border);border-radius:8px;padding:0.75rem">
          <strong>📋 Compliance</strong>
          <p style="font-size:0.78rem;margin:0.25rem 0 0;color:var(--text-3)">PII detection, regulatory adherence</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Severity Levels</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Block:</strong> Immediately reject the request/response</li>
        <li><strong>Warn:</strong> Flag for review but allow through</li>
        <li><strong>Log:</strong> Record for audit without interrupting</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Configuration</h2>
      <p>Configure guardrails via the <a href="/guardrails" style="color:#06b6d4">Guardrails</a> page. Each guardrail can be individually enabled/disabled and its severity level adjusted.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- ── Tools & MCP ── -->
    <div id="doc-tools" class="docs-section">
      <div class="docs-breadcrumb"><a href="/docs">Docs</a> / Core Features</div>
      <h1>Tools &amp; MCP</h1>
      <p>Agents extend their capabilities through tools — functions they can invoke during the ReAct reasoning loop. The platform supports both built-in tools (hosted in the Tools Service) and external tools via the Model Context Protocol (MCP).</p>

      <h2>Tool Execution Flow</h2>
      <div class="docs-diagram">
        <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A["🤖 LLM Response"] --> B{"🔧 Tool call<br/>in output?"}
    B -->|"No"| C["📡 Stream to user"]
    B -->|"Yes"| D["⚙️ Agent Service<br/>parses tool call"]
    D --> E{"Built-in<br/>or MCP?"}
    E -->|"Built-in"| F["⚙️ Tools Service<br/>(sandboxed container)"]
    E -->|"MCP"| G["🌐 MCP Server<br/>(external)"]
    F --> H["📋 Tool result JSON"]
    G --> H
    H --> I["🤖 Re-inject into<br/>LLM context"]
    I --> A
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Built-in Tools</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Security</th></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Web Search</strong></td><td>Search query string</td><td>Top results with titles, URLs, snippets</td><td>Rate-limited, DuckDuckGo API</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Calculator</strong></td><td>Math expression</td><td>Numeric result</td><td>Safe eval — no exec/import</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Document Lookup</strong></td><td>Natural language query</td><td>Top-k relevant document chunks</td><td>Read-only ChromaDB access</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Note Taking</strong></td><td>Action (save/list) + content</td><td>Saved note or note list</td><td>File-scoped, container-local</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>HTTP Request</strong></td><td>URL + method + body</td><td>Response body (truncated)</td><td>Container-isolated, timeout-limited</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>MCP Integration</h2>
      <p>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) allows agents to discover and call tools hosted on external MCP-compliant servers. The platform acts as an <strong>MCP client</strong>.</p>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Protocol</td><td>JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP/SSE</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Discovery</td><td>Agents call <code>tools/list</code> on MCP servers to enumerate available tools</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Invocation</td><td>Agent Service proxies tool calls to the configured MCP server endpoint</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Configuration</td><td>Register MCP servers via the <a href="/mcp" style="color:#06b6d4">MCP page</a> (name, URL, auth)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Security</td><td>Optional API key auth, configurable timeout, response size limits</td></tr>
      </table>

      <div class="docs-callout info">
        <strong>Sandboxing:</strong> All built-in tool execution happens inside the Tools Service container — a separate process from the Agent Service. This provides container-level isolation: a misbehaving tool cannot crash the agent, leak memory, or access agent data.
      </div>
    </div>

