      <h1>Architecture Decision Records</h1>
      <p>Each ADR captures a significant design choice, its context, alternatives considered, and rationale.</p>

      <h2>ADR Index</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th>ID</th><th>Decision</th><th>Principles</th></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-001</td><td>LangGraph over AgentExecutor</td><td>AP-7, AP-5</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-002</td><td>SQLite for Config and Memory</td><td>AP-3, AP-10</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-003</td><td>Separate Tools Service</td><td>AP-4, AP-7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-004</td><td>Multi-Provider LLM Switching</td><td>AP-2, AP-1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-005</td><td>ChromaDB for Vector Storage</td><td>AP-3, AP-8</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-006</td><td>Three-Pipeline Observability</td><td>AP-5, AP-10</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-007</td><td>EJS + CSS Custom Properties</td><td>AP-3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-008</td><td>Guardrails as Graph Gates</td><td>AP-4, AP-9</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-009</td><td>A2A and MCP Protocols</td><td>AP-6</td></tr>
        <tr><td>ADR-010</td><td>Thin UI Proxy Pattern</td><td>AP-1, AP-7</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-001 · LangGraph over AgentExecutor</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>The agent needs a multi-step reasoning loop with explicit tool execution, guardrail gates, and retrieval context injection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Use LangGraph <code>StateGraph</code> with explicit nodes (<code>retrieve_context</code>, <code>reason</code>, <code>execute_tools</code>, <code>generate_response</code>) and conditional edges.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) LangChain AgentExecutor — simpler but opaque. (2) Custom async loop — full control but no graph visualisation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Full visibility into each step. Guardrails inserted at any edge. State is typed (<code>AgentState</code>). Trade-off: more boilerplate.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-002 · SQLite for Config and Memory</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>The platform stores agents, skills, prompts, guardrails, custom tools, A2A peers, MCP servers, conversation history, and session summaries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Use SQLite at <code>/data/memory.db</code> with thread-local connections and recovery from stale handles.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) PostgreSQL — production-grade but adds complexity. (2) In-memory dicts — no persistence. (3) Redis — poor for relational queries.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Zero-config persistence. Single-file backup. No network latency. Limitation: single-writer, unsuitable for horizontal scaling.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-003 · Separate Tools Service</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>Agent tools execute untrusted operations. Running them in the agent process creates security and stability risks.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Run tools in an isolated <code>tools-service</code> (FastAPI, port 8001). Agent calls tools via HTTP.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) In-process — simpler but crashes affect agent. (2) Subprocess — isolation without a service boundary.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Tool crashes don't affect reasoning. Independent scaling. ~5ms HTTP overhead. <strong>Exception:</strong> <code>vector_search</code>/<code>vector_store</code> run in-process for latency.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-004 · Multi-Provider LLM Switching</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>Different use cases require different LLM providers: Ollama for offline, Azure OpenAI for compliance, OpenAI for latest models.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Abstract all providers behind LangChain's <code>BaseChatModel</code>. Runtime switching via <code>POST /models/switch</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) Hardcoded Ollama. (2) Config file per environment. (3) LiteLLM proxy — overkill for 4 providers.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Provider switch is a single API call. New providers need one <code>elif</code> branch. Active model is global state (single-writer).</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-005 · ChromaDB for Vector Storage</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>RAG requires a vector database. The platform must start with a single <code>docker-compose up</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>ChromaDB in HTTP mode (internal port 8000, external port 8200) with LangChain wrapper and Ollama embeddings.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) Pinecone — requires cloud account. (2) Weaviate — heavier. (3) FAISS — no persistence. (4) pgvector — requires PostgreSQL.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Simple container, cosine search, collection isolation. Embeddings tied to model — switching requires re-ingestion.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-006 · Three-Pipeline Observability</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>AI debugging requires three data types: LLM traces, quantitative metrics, and log aggregation. No single tool covers all.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Three parallel pipelines: Langfuse for LLM traces, Prometheus for metrics, Loki for logs. OTel Collector routes telemetry. Grafana unifies.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) Langfuse only — no metrics/logs. (2) Jaeger + Prometheus — no LLM cost tracking. (3) Datadog — SaaS cost.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Full coverage. Six additional containers. All optional — agent degrades to no-op if keys absent.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-007 · EJS + CSS Custom Properties</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>UI needs 23 pages with light/dark theme, fast SSR, and zero build step.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Express.js + EJS with CSS custom properties. Theme toggle via <code>.dark</code> class on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) React/Next.js — adds build pipeline. (2) HTMX — less ecosystem. (3) Svelte — requires compilation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Zero build step. &lt;50ms SSR. Theme tokens enforced via Copilot skill. Trade-off: no component model.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-008 · Guardrails as Graph Gates</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>Safety checks need to run at specific pipeline points — before LLM calls and after response generation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Guardrails as functions within graph nodes, not FastAPI middleware. Input in <code>reason()</code>, output in <code>generate_response()</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) FastAPI middleware — no agent state access. (2) Separate service — adds latency. (3) LLM-based — expensive and recursive.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Full <code>AgentState</code> access. Can short-circuit graph. Regex-based detection is fast (&lt;1ms). Config in SQLite.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-009 · A2A and MCP Protocols</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>Platform needs to interact with external agents and tool servers. Proprietary APIs would create vendor lock-in.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td>Implement Google's A2A for agent communication and Anthropic's MCP for tool discovery. Both use JSON-over-HTTP.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) Custom RPC — non-standard. (2) gRPC — harder to debug. (3) GraphQL — overkill for task delegation.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Any framework can register as A2A peer. Any MCP server can be added. Interoperable by default. Trade-off: specs still evolving.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <h2>ADR-010 · Thin UI Proxy Pattern</h2>
      <table class="docs-table">
        <tr><th style="width:120px">Field</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Context</td><td>Browser-to-service calls fail due to CORS and internal Docker networking.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Decision</td><td><code>ui-console</code> acts as a thin API proxy. All <code>/api/*</code> routes forward to backend services. Zero business logic.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Alternatives</td><td>(1) API Gateway — heavyweight. (2) Nginx — static config. (3) Direct browser calls — blocked by Docker networking.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consequences</td><td>Single entry point (port 3000). CORS handled once. ~2ms latency. <strong>Note:</strong> n8n auth logic lives in proxy — see AP-1.</td></tr>
      </table>

      <div class="docs-callout tip">
        <strong>Full details:</strong> See <code>docs/DECISIONS.md</code> for complete ADR records with dates, status, and principle cross-references.
      </div>
    </div>

